# Hotel Booking System

## Stack
- ASP.NET Core MVC (.NET 9) + EF Core SQL Server
- React/Vite SPA only for Room Management (`ClientApp` → `wwwroot/room-management`)
- Customer site = Razor + `booking.css` / `booking-ui.js` (not React unless asked)

## Brand
- Public hotel name: **Mori International Hotel**
- Guest/public site = `_CustomerLayout` + **Booking** (`/` / `/Booking`)
- One guest book flow only: book ahead → auto reservation; near arrival (min lead time) → booking
- Do not add a separate Reserve page/route
- Admin staff shell = `_Layout` (`/Rooms`, etc.) — label as Admin when needed

## Surfaces
- **Admin**: `Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml` + sidebar (Home, Guest, Rooms, Privacy)
- **Guest/public**: `Views/Shared/_CustomerLayout.cshtml` + `BookingController` (default route)
- **Rooms data**: `IRoomService` / `RoomDto` / `RoomStatus` (Available | Unavailable)
- **Booking page** must show rooms with `Status == Available` from Room Management

## Design (customer UI)
- Colors only: `--navy`, `--white`, `--teal` (and soft variants already in CSS)
- Hero photo: `/Images/moriyama.jpg` on `.guest-hero` only — never full-page background
- Keep UI scalable: fluid `clamp()`, `auto-fit` grids, no horizontal overflow
- Prefer UI-only first (modals, toasts, validation messages) unless user asks for persistence

## Do / Don't
- Do reuse existing services, DTOs, CSS variables, and layouts
- Do keep admin and customer shells separate
- Don't invent a second design system or purple/cream AI themes
- Don't add booking DB/API until the user asks for real save/submit behavior
- Don't expand scope beyond the request; match existing file patterns
